TYPE ERROR: Foreign declaration 'Ref' redeclares a built-in runtime name at a type the built-in does not have: the built-in is 'a -> Ref a', this declaration claims '(Unit -> <IO> Unit) -> Ref (Unit -> Unit)'. A local extern whose name matches a stdlib/runtime.mdk built-in is always lowered as that built-in, whatever the local signature says, so its callers would be typed against a signature the called code does not honour. A redeclaration may fix the built-in's type variables and authorities, and must otherwise be its type. Rename the extern to a name the runtime does not already define, or declare it at the built-in's own signature
TYPE ERROR: Foreign declaration 'arrayMakeWith' redeclares a built-in runtime name with a NARROWER effect row: the built-in performs <IO>, this declaration claims <>, which does not cover <IO>. A local extern whose name matches a stdlib/runtime.mdk built-in is always lowered as that built-in, whatever the local signature says — so 'arrayMakeWith' really does perform <IO>, and every caller typechecked against this declaration would be told it does not. Declare the built-in's own row `<IO>` (a WIDER row such as `<IO>` is also accepted — over-declaring is safe), or rename the extern to a name the runtime does not already define
TYPE ERROR: Foreign declaration 'writeFile' redeclares a built-in runtime name with a NARROWER effect row: the built-in performs <FileWrite path>, this declaration claims <>, which does not cover <FileWrite path>. A local extern whose name matches a stdlib/runtime.mdk built-in is always lowered as that built-in, whatever the local signature says — so 'writeFile' really does perform <FileWrite path>, and every caller typechecked against this declaration would be told it does not. Declare the built-in's own row `<FileWrite path>` (a WIDER row such as `<IO>` is also accepted — over-declaring is safe), or rename the extern to a name the runtime does not already define
